1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:12,774
Paying a team in Zcash usually means a transaction per person. Devoid pays the whole list in one shielded transaction. Let me show you a payout, start to finish.

2
00:00:13,467 --> 00:00:21,363
It's a non-custodial wallet that runs entirely in your browser. No account, and no server holding your keys.

3
00:00:22,067 --> 00:00:33,827
Here's the catch. Shielding hides amounts and recipients, but the number of outputs is public, and the fee follows from it. So a payout still hints at how many people you paid.

4
00:00:34,533 --> 00:00:39,621
This is a demo wallet on a simulated chain. Play money, real app.

5
00:00:40,333 --> 00:00:48,805
Let's pay three contributors. I'll paste the list into "Send" and hit "Check recipients". Every address is validated first.

6
00:00:49,500 --> 00:01:00,804
Now the part I like most. Tick "Hide how many people you are paying", and Devoid pads the transaction to five payment slots, with the extra fee shown up front.

7
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:09,684
The review spells it out: three recipients, five outputs on chain, the exact fee, and what an observer can still see.

8
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:19,448
I'll confirm with the passphrase. Here the send is simulated; on a real wallet, this same screen builds, proves and broadcasts it.

9
00:01:20,133 --> 00:01:27,237
In "Activity" it reads "Sent to 3 recipients". The padding is yours, so it never counts as a payment.

10
00:01:27,933 --> 00:01:40,461
And it's real. One Rust engine on librustzcash runs natively and as WebAssembly. Both have sent shielded payments mined on mainnet, and padding is verified on testnet.

11
00:01:41,917 --> 00:01:49,261
One transaction, multiple payments, and fewer clues on chain. Devoid is open source, on GitHub.
